# .gitleaksignore — accepted findings, one fingerprint per line.
# AI Security Roadmap control 4.1.
#
# A fingerprint identifies ONE finding: <commit>:<file>:<rule-id>:<line>
# (copy it verbatim from the pipeline log or the gitleaks-report.json artifact).
# Use this file only for:
#   * a historical secret that has been ROTATED/REVOKED and cannot be removed
#     from history without a rewrite the team has decided not to do;
#   * a confirmed false positive that is too specific for a .gitleaks.toml
#     allowlist.
# Every fingerprint is preceded by a comment LINE: what it was, why it is safe
# now, who accepted it and when. gitleaks does NOT accept trailing comments on
# the fingerprint line itself (verified with 8.30.1 — the entry is silently
# ignored). Never add a fingerprint for a secret that is still valid.
#
# Example:
# dev Mailgun key, revoked 2026-09-22 (Jose); value now injected by CI variable
# 3f2a9c1e0b7d4a5f6c8e9d0a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c:src/config/dev.env:generic-api-key:12

# ── Accepted findings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# None. gitleaks 8.30.1 over the full history of origin/master on 2026-11-05
# (337 commits, upstream Log4Qt history included), with the same config the
# SecretScan stage uses (this repo's .gitleaks.toml + the DisplayNote
# dn-credential-call-argument rule), reports no leaks.
